"lang":"Serbian","meaning":"Field of blackbirds","kind":"Region","lat":42.55,"lng":20.833,"wiki":"Kosovo"}],"langNotes":{"Irish":"Gaelic place names (Dubh, Baile, Kil-) cluster on the island of Ireland and traveled with emigrants to North America and Australia.","Old Norse":"The Vikings' language: endings like -vik (bay), -by (village) and -borg trace the North Atlantic islands and the Nordic coasts.","Old English":"English endings such as -ford (crossing), -pool and -ham (village) show up almost only in England and its colonies.","French":"The footprint of French colonization: Quebec, the Mississippi basin (Detroit, Baton Rouge), and West and Central Africa.","Miami-Illinois":"Native American names left in the Great Lakes region and the upper Mississippi valley.","Spanish":"Spain's American empire: saints' names (San/Santa) and religious words spread from California and Mexico through Central and South America.","Algonquin":"The Algonquian languages ranged across eastern Canada and the Great Lakes; the Ottawa River region still carries their names.","Hawaiian":"A Polynesian language: names are vowel-rich with few consonants, found across Pacific islands.","Portuguese":"Portugal's Age of Discovery left names along the Atlantic: Brazil, the Gulf of Guinea, Cape Verde and the Moroccan coast.","Nahuatl":"The language of the Aztecs; endings like -co and -tlan cluster in central and southern Mexico.","Shona":"A Bantu language spoken mainly on the Zimbabwe plateau.","English":"Many English place names were given by recent colonists: names built from words like \"freedom\" or \"Christ\" are common in colonies.","Arabic":"Arabic place names (al-, Dar, Jabal) followed the spread of Islam and are densest across North Africa, the Middle East, southern Spain and the Persian Gulf.","Maasai":"An East African Nilotic language, spoken by the Maasai in the Rift Valley of Kenya and Tanzania.","Amharic":"A Semitic language of Ethiopia, spoken almost only on the Ethiopian highlands.","Venetian":"The Republic of Venice once ruled the eastern Adriatic coast; Monte Negro is a name it left behind.","Serbian":"A South Slavic language spoken mainly in Serbia and around the Balkans.","Greek":"Greek reached across the eastern Mediterranean and the Black Sea coast, including Bulgaria, Turkey and southern Italy.","Old Germanic":"Early Germanic place names; -burg (fortress) is most common in German-speaking lands and around Luxembourg.","Latin":"The legacy of Rome: forms like Nova, Trans- and -ia are found across Europe and in later colonies.","German":"German endings -burg, -bruck and -berg cluster in Germany, Austria and Switzerland.","Old High German":"Medieval southern German; names with -furt (ford) and München-type forms sit in southern and central Germany.","Old Dutch":"Early names of the Low Countries; endings like -dam and -brug are strung along the Dutch lowlands.","Ancient Greek":"Names ending in -polis (city), like Neapolis and Tripolis, mark cities Greek colonists founded around the Mediterranean.","Ukrainian":"An East Slavic language; names often end in -ль or -ів and sit in Ukraine.","Russian":"Towns ending in -град or -ск line the Trans-Siberian Railway and Russian expansion all the way to the Pacific.","Old East Slavic":"Names from medieval Rus; Novgorod (\"new city\") is one of Russia's oldest cities.","Medieval Greek":"Byzantine-era Greek, found mainly in today's Turkey and the Balkans.","Georgian":"A Kartvelian language of the South Caucasus, used almost only in Georgia.","Phoenician":"The Semitic language of the Phoenicians; names trace the Lebanese coast and their Mediterranean colonies.","Hebrew":"Modern Hebrew place names are concentrated in Israel.","Urdu":"Urdu is the national language of Pakistan and is also widely spoken in northern India.","Urdu / Persian":"Persian words entered South Asia with the Mughal Empire; endings like -stan and -abad run on into Central Asia.","Sanskrit":"The Sanskrit cultural sphere: India, Nepal and the Indianized cities of Southeast Asia (-pura means \"city\").","Marathi":"The language of Maharashtra in western India.","Persian / Urdu":"The ending -abad (dwelling, city) is a mark of Persian influence, common in South and Central Asia.","Malay":"Malay names (Kuala for river mouth, Pulau for island) cluster on the Malay Peninsula and the Indonesian archipelago.","Burmese":"A Sino-Tibetan language of the Burmish branch, used only in Myanmar and its borderlands.","Khmer":"The language of Cambodia; Phnom (hill) is the Khmer hallmark.","Lao":"The language of Laos; Viang (city) appears in Laos and northeastern Thailand.","Mongolian":"Names from the Mongolian plateau: Ulaan (red) and Khot (city) appear in Mongolia and Inner Mongolia.","Turkic":"The Turkic languages stretch west along the Central Asian steppe: -kent (city), -tash (stone).","Turkmen / Persian":"A Persianized Turkic region along the Karakum Desert in southern Central Asia.","Tajik":"Tajik is a form of Persian, spoken in the mountains of southern Central Asia.","Lenape":"The language of the Delaware people; Native place names are scattered across the northeastern United States.","Sinhala":"The main language of Sri Lanka."}}